;; C's negative controls (doc/GATE-SURVEY.md, GTS81 (a)): each is a crossed pair, the subject's run (or read) judged by the
;; level's own judge against the reference's expected result, which it must fail. No program or file of its own: two of the
;; corpus, both passing in the records (test/c/check-linux-amd64.edn, test/c/check-dg-linux-amd64.edn). A control that
;; passes (the judge passes what differs) or is not judged while its subject was (the reference has no expected result)
;; fails the judgment; one whose subject or reference is not in the run (--corpus, --only, a subset) or was not built, run
;; or read is left (its own row says so). No control is a row of the results, the records, the classes or the counts.
;;   :level :run  bin/ci check's judge (arbace.c.run/result, through controls-failing) and bin/ci check --aot's (cf2g's judge,
;;                the same rule): the subject's node and run against the reference's gcc run (test/c/ref-gcc15.edn)
;;   :level :dg   bin/ci dg's judge (arbace.c.dg/verdict, then message-verdict, as a row): the subject's read against the
;;                reference's directives; both in test/c/essential-dg.txt, so --essential judges it too
;; Read with the Clojure reader.
[{:id "_control/fannkuchredux-vs-fasta" :level :run :subject "benchmarksgame/fannkuchredux.c" :reference "benchmarksgame/fasta.c"
  :why "the stdout comparison: status 0 both, fannkuchredux's output against fasta's digest (differ stdout)"}
 {:id "_control/bitint-75-vs-attr-syntax-3" :level :dg :subject "gcc-bitint/gcc.dg/bitint-75.c" :reference "gcc-c23/gcc.dg/c23-attr-syntax-3.c"
  :why "the line judge: bitint-75's read (an error on line 10, \"impossible constraint\") against c23-attr-syntax-3's dg-error lines (7, 9, 14, ...): missed and extra"}]
